WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.231
Ethereal and fleeting, mesmerizing and elusive,
these floating blue flames have been a mainstay

00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:10.340
in European folklore since at least the 14th
century.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.960
You may have heard the name Will-o’-the-Wisp,
but do you know what it is?

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.840
Stories of sentient blue flames floating and
bouncing across marshes and bogs permeate

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.390
cultures from northern Europe to Australia.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.470
While some stories have faded into ephemera
just as the floating blue flames that inspired

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:34.989
them, these elusive creatures remain mainstays
of myth and legend in pockets across the globe.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.720
So what are they?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.720
Faeries?

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.720
Fireballs?

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:39.720
Ghosts?

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.720
Atmospheric phenomena?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.660
Here’s the haunting history of the Will-o’-the-Wisp.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.920
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.590
The Will-o’-the-Wisp are most frequently
described as small, moving blue flames that

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.280
hover a few feet off the ground and do not
flicker.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.691
Cold or cool to the touch -- if you’re brave
or reckless enough to touch one—they can

00:01:01.691 --> 00:01:05.010
be found in marshlands, bogs, and swamps.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.689
All treacherous places to wander even without
the alluring glow of the Will-o’-the-Wisp

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:11.840
leading you deeper into danger.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.400
Follow the Will-o’-the-Wisp’s seductive
dance, and you may find hidden treasure…

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.560
or a watery grave.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.020
Stories of these elusive and inscrutable flames
vary.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.070
Some say spotting the floating flame is an
indication that someone is blessed with the

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:32.409
power of foresight, but more often, folklore
and literature paints them as impish tricksters,

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:34.909
restless spirits, or malevolent beings.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:39.700
In Sweden, Will-o’-the-Wisps are ghosts
with lanterns fated to wander aimlessly for

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.930
removing their neighbours’ boundary markers.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.970
In English tradition, the blue flames are
lights carried by elves.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:52.470
And in Australia, the light may approach someone,
but anyone who tries to catch it will disappear.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:58.270
Other tales say the lights are crafted by
fairies, or even a fire species all its own.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.130
In Belgium and the Netherlands, they are the
lost spirits of unbaptized children, while

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:08.410
in other places the floating flames are said
to be the wandering souls of unhappy women,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:14.099
unrighteous men, priests who have broken their
vow of chastity, or even those souls who escaped

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:15.099
purgatory.

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:20.370
In fact, the ethereal light gleans its most
enduring name Will-o’-the-Wisp or “Will

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.570
with a Wisp” from the Saxon word “wile”
for fraud, trick, or deceit, and the Swedish

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.980
word “wisp” meaning a small lit bundle
of tinder.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.209
Variations include ‘Will-with-the-Wisp,’
‘Willy Wisp,’ and ‘Will o’ the Wykes.”

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:38.590
Essentially, the name hints that the
light comes from some trickster spirit holding

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.150
a flame aloft.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:44.360
Further north, in Norwegian folklore the Will-’o-the-Wisp
is called “Hoberdy’s

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.849
Lantern,” “Hobany’s Lantern,” or even
“Hob and his Lantern.”

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:55.610
‘Hob’ may come from the Norwegian hoppe meaning mare, or the Old Norse word hoppa which translates to leap or hop.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.519
Which might not be as obvious a name as a
deceitful bundle of tinder, until you know

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:04.050
that the Will-o’-the-Wisp’s movement often
resembles the cantering motion of a horse.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.160
Yet another moniker for the floating lights?

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.209
“Jacky Lantern” or “Jack-o’-Lantern.”

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:13.780
Yes, that’s right —the carved pumpkins
that adorn many a stoop or doorstep on Halloween

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.280
actually borrow their name from this mysterious
light.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.150
Jack-o’-lantern is of English language origin
and simply means Jack of the Lantern.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.000
So when did we start hearing about these creatures?

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.260
Well, the first recorded sighting of Will-o’-the-Wisp
is described in a 1340 text penned by Welsh

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.489
poet Dafydd ap Gwilym.
He wrote that “in every hollow” live “a

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:37.060
hundred wrymouthed wisps.”

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.870
He names the phenomenon canwyll corff, Welsh for “corpse-candle,” thereby introducing

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.970
what will become another common name for the
supernatural blue flames, and associating

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.970
the lights with death and burial sites.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.750
The first appearance of the lights in the
English language occurs in 1563 (Book of Meteors

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.860
by William Fulke) under the name “ignis
fatuus.”

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.050
Here they are described as “foolish fire
that hurteth not.”

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.641
In another variation of the Will-o’-the-Wisp
myth, the ghost lights are attributed to a

00:04:05.641 --> 00:04:11.250
mischievous sprite called a Puck, which uses
the light to lure humans to fall into ditches,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.700
bogs, and pools and then laughs at their predicament
before fleeing.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.530
Sometimes referred to in this form as “walking
fire” it can also appear as a horse, bull,

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:24.360
or eagle, but it is always a trickster.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.290
Shakespeare adopts the name Puck for the character
of a shape-shifting sprite in A Midsummer

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:33.130
Night’s Dream, first performed around 1596.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:38.030
This Puck turns into a phosphorescent glow
and hovers over the marshes at night to trick

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:39.100
travelers.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:44.919
In Henry IV he uses the name ignis fatuus
to refer to “a ball of wildfire.”

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:49.900
The Will-o’-the-Wisp even appears in John
Milton’s famous epic poem Paradise Lost

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:55.950
(1667) as a malevolent distraction: “a wandring
fire/Compact of unctuous vapour, which the

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:02.200
night/Condenses, and the cold environs round/Kindl’d
through agitation to a flame,/Which oft, they

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:08.080
say, some evil spirit attends,/ Hovering and
blazing with delusive light,/Misleads th’amaz’d

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.240
night-wanderer from his way,/ To bogs and
mires, and oft through pond or pool,/There

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.300
swallow’d up and lost, from succour far...”

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.380
Like many other references in literature and
folklore, Milton’s Will-o’-the-Wisp is

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.699
associated with danger, bad news, and even
death.

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:26.280
But over time, the elusive blue flame also
became a literary metaphor for anything that

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:32.320
is just out of reach, a delusion one may chase
endlessly but unsuccessfully.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.722
Will-o’-the-Wisp sightings are reported
across the globe, and the frequency of their

00:05:35.722 --> 00:05:41.190
marshland appearances points to a very real
origin—the stories may be made up, but the

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.030
lights themselves are not.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.150
There’s scientific truth to these reports.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:50.259
Theories explaining the Will-’o-the-Wisp
phenomenon circulated in scientific communities

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:52.130
for hundreds of years.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:58.360
In fact, in 1704 Isaac Newton wrote in Opticks
of the “ignis fatuus” as “vapours shining

00:05:58.360 --> 00:05:59.850
without heat.”

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:04.410
The vague statement unsurprisingly sparked
vigorous debate in the academic communities

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.449
of the 18th and 19th centuries.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:11.450
English folklorist and historian Jabez Allies travelled over Europe collecting

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:17.169
stories and evidence of antiquity—and in
1839 he got lucky with his own first-hand

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:20.699
experience catching a glimpse of the mysterious
flame.

00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:26.009
Allies claims that while traveling near Worcestershire
in England he saw the Will-o’-the-Wisp.

00:06:26.009 --> 00:06:30.680
He described the light as “very clear and
strong,” unwavering, and “much bluer”

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.800
in color than a candle.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.600
He wrote: “Sometimes it was only like a
flash in the pan on the ground; at other times

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.090
it rose up several feet and fell to the earth,
and became extinguished; and many times it

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:48.479
proceeded horizontally from fifty to one hundred
yards with an undulating motion, like the

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:53.250
flight of the green woodpecker, and about
as rapid; and once or twice it proceeded with

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.750
considerable rapidity in a straight line upon
or close to the ground.”

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:02.099
Allies reports that the two nights in a row
he saw the phenomenon there were only minimal

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:04.340
clouds and bright starlight.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.940
Both evenings were “rather warm” and had
no fog.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:12.569
He speculates that the Will-o’-the-Wisps
appear on nights after a rainfall in the winter

00:07:12.569 --> 00:07:13.569
season.

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:15.340
Was Allies on to something?

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.060
What else could explain these odd flames?

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.060
Fireflies?

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:23.389
Too small, and they glow with a yellow or
green flickering light.

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:24.520
Glow-worms?

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.139
The females do produce a bright light but
don’t fly.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:30.639
Male glow-worms fly—but don’t glow.

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:32.740
St. Elmo’s fire perhaps?

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:37.639
The plasma-creating weather phenomenon does
produce a blue glow, though it requires a

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:41.940
strong electrical field in the atmosphere
and an encounter with a conductor like the

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:46.640
mast of a ship or the wing of a plane, which
doesn’t appear in reported Will-o’-the-Wisp

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:47.849
sightings.

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:49.210
What about ball lightning?

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:54.180
Spherical, glowing, and capable of lasting
for longer than a brief flash, yes.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.759
But only during thunderstorms, with ranges
in color, and usually accompanied by a hissing

00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:01.820
sound and a distinct odor.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:06.099
Will-o’-the-Wisp aren’t associated with
any smell and only seem to appear a day or

00:08:06.099 --> 00:08:08.729
more after rainfall or storm.

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:12.250
Perhaps then, dare I say it—luminous owls?

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:16.900
An unusual occurrence in which the feathers
of the bird are contaminated by a fungus whose

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:21.340
metabolic process produces a glow when the
owl is in the dark.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:25.520
But Will-o’-the-Wisps move more slowly than
owls typically hunt and don’t tend to cover

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.080
much ground.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.520
The most likely explanation?

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.779
Spontaneous combustion of marsh or swamp gas,
a rationale proposed at least as early as

00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:41.580
1729 by W. Derham who, after observing the
phenomenon over half a decade, directly refuted

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:45.950
the hypothesis that the lights were caused
by glowworms flying together.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:50.040
Given the frequently reported occurrence of
the Will-o’-the-Wisp on marshland, swamps,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.750
and bogs, swamp gas seems pretty plausible.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:58.899
Made up of about 60 percent methane as well
as other components like carbon dioxide, swamp

00:08:58.899 --> 00:09:05.970
gas is the byproduct of decomposing vegetation
in areas like swamps, marshes, and even landfills.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:10.700
And in some rare cases, if there is enough
heat and oxygen produced by the metabolic

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:16.180
process, the gas can spontaneously combust
and produce fire.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:20.019
And methane just happens to burn with a blue
flame and a yellowish glow.

00:09:20.019 --> 00:09:24.390
Will the mystery of the Will-o’-the-Wisp
ever truly be solved?

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:30.240
Even today many people swear the Will-o’-the-Wisp
is a supernatural or paranormal experience.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.920
Interestingly, there have been no modern encounters
with the Will-o’-the-Wisp and therefore

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.010
no new data to add to any potential debunking
theories.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.630
Perhaps there are no more Will-o’-the-Wisps
because we have drained and demolished so

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.180
many marshes and lowlands.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:49.820
Maybe we’ll never know what caused the glowing
blue flames spotted so many years ago.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.540
And I kind of like that.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.860
Glow on mysterious fiery orbs.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:04.679
Glow on.

